Context: in 'Structure à l'infini des M_{g,ν}' Grothendieck wants to describe the homotopy type, or the topos, of a space stratified by a normal-crossings divisor. The model case is the Deligne–Mumford compactification, whose boundary divisor has normal crossings. The method is 'dévissage': reconstruct the space from elementary local pieces and canonical maps between them. These pieces are the strata and their tubular neighbourhoods, with parts of the boundary deleted. On the previous page he defined general 'tube' topoi V^{B(*)}_{A,R} as fibre products, indexed by flags of strata with codimensions d ≥ d' ≥ d''. On the next page he reconstructs the global opens by gluing.

On this page he first says it seems plausible that these 'types', once listed, admit correspondences among themselves that should be made explicit (referring to pp. 74' ff.). He then works out the 'cas-type' n = 2: X = C^2 with divisor the two coordinate axes. Here ℙ denotes a point, not the projective line, and D is the disc.

The strata are:
- D_0^* = C*×C*, the open stratum, with D_0 = X;
- Δ_1^* = C*×pt and Δ_2^* = pt×C*, the open parts of the axes Δ_1 = C×pt and Δ_2 = pt×C, with D_1 = Δ_1 ⊔ Δ_2;
- D_2 = pt, the origin.

For each incidence (S ⊂ closure of T) he lists the tubular neighbourhood U_{S,T} and its punctured version U^*_{S,T}, with homotopy-level product models:
- U_{Δ_1^*,X} = C*×D and U^* = C*×D*;
- U_{Δ_2^*,X} = D×C* and U^* = D*×C*;
- U_{pt,Δ_1} = D×pt and U^* = D*×pt;
- U_{pt,Δ_2} = pt×D and U^* = pt×D*;
- U_{pt,X} = D×D, with the partially punctured versions U^{Δ_1}_{pt,X} = D×D* and U^{Δ_2}_{pt,X} = D*×D, and the fully punctured U^*_{pt,X} = D*×D*.

The count is 4 strata + 5 incidences × 2 (full and punctured) + 2 partially punctured corner pieces = 16. This matches the '4×4 = 16 types' he announces. These are the elementary building blocks whose canonical maps (restrictions, inclusions) are then used on the next page to glue C^2, C^2 minus an axis, C^2 minus the origin, and so on.

The left-margin note (hard to read) appears to warn that these writings are only heuristic, i.e. product models up to homotopy. It seems to say that replacing them by correct formulas would require more work.
